proof_bodies['n11-t4-cubic']=r'<p>A pure fourth-order anticoherent eleven-qubit state would produce a positive, rotation-invariant three-copy operator \(X\) with \(\operatorname{Tr}X=1\). Its low-rank multipoles impose twenty-seven necessary equations on twenty-seven real block coordinates. Their integer matrix \(A\) satisfies \(\det A\equiv3\pmod{37}\), so it is invertible. The equations force \(X=0\), a contradiction. Taking real parts of positive blocks handles arbitrary complex states.</p><p>A second exact certificate expresses \(\lVert\psi\rVert^6\) as a sum of twenty-six cubic multipole invariants. Every term contains a rank between one and four, so fourth-order anticoherence would force \(1=0\). Its full polynomial expansion is verified coefficientwise with rational arithmetic.</p><p>This proves that the maximal order at \(n=11\) is three. Combined with the smaller-size exclusions and a twelve-qubit witness, it also proves that twelve is the first qubit number admitting a pure fourth-order anticoherent state.</p><p><a href="./research/reported-exclusions/index.html#n11">Read both proofs and their exact certificates ↗</a> · <a href="./research/reported-exclusions/n11-t4/formula.tex">LaTeX proof</a> · <a href="./research/reported-exclusions/n11-t4/verify.py">Polynomial verifier</a></p>'
